      {
         "id": "d",
         "prompt": "Use Euler's method, starting at \\( x = 0 \\) with two steps of size \\( 0.25 \\), to approximate \\( f(0.5) \\). Given that \\( f''(x) > 0 \\) for all \\( x \\), is this approximation an overestimate or an underestimate of \\( f(0.5) \\)? Give a reason for your answer.",
         "setup_required": false,
         "answer_latex": "f(0.5) \\approx \\frac{73}{16}, \\text{ an underestimate}",
         "worked_solution": [
            {
               "text": "First step from (0, 3), slope 3.",
               "latex": "f(0.25) \\approx 3 + 0.25(3) = 3.75"
            },
            {
               "text": "Second step from (0.25, 3.75), slope 3.75 - 0.5 = 3.25.",
               "latex": "f(0.5) \\approx 3.75 + 0.25(3.25) = 4.5625"
            },
            {
               "text": "The graph of f is concave up, so each Euler segment, which follows a tangent direction, lies below the curve.",
               "latex": "f''(x) > 0"
            },
            {
               "text": "So the approximation is an underestimate of f(0.5).",
               "latex": "f(0.5) \\approx \\frac{73}{16}"
            }
